WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.620
The model says cells compare their signals.

2
00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:07.840
How do we know real cells do?

3
00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:11.380
By the late 1980s, one thing was clear.

4
00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:15.620
Embryos lacking Notch or Delta make too many neural cells.

5
00:00:16.633 --> 00:00:20.793
What was not clear was how two neighbours, both carrying the receptor and the

6
00:00:20.793 --> 00:00:22.073
ligand, make the choice.

7
00:00:23.033 --> 00:00:24.433
Start on the embryo map.

8
00:00:25.493 --> 00:00:29.193
Fly embryos lacking Notch or Delta make far too many neuroblasts.

9
00:00:30.167 --> 00:00:32.927
They also make too little epidermis on their underside.

10
00:00:33.967 --> 00:00:36.167
Genes like these are called neurogenic.

11
00:00:36.987 --> 00:00:39.707
Without the receptor, no cell can be told, not you.

12
00:00:40.667 --> 00:00:46.807
Now the notum. In 1991, Pascal Heitzler and Pat Simpson made fly mosaics.

13
00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:52.690
Patches of mutant cells, or cells with a different level of Notch activity, lay

14
00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:53.810
beside normal cells.

15
00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.500
Then they asked which cells at the patch borders became precursors.

16
00:00:59.433 --> 00:01:02.013
Cells mutant for Notch became neural on their own.

17
00:01:02.967 --> 00:01:07.567
Predict. What about cells mutant for Delta, beside normal cells?

18
00:01:14.017 --> 00:01:15.937
They did not become neural on their own.

19
00:01:16.757 --> 00:01:20.117
They still had Notch, so normal neighbours could still say, not you.

20
00:01:21.077 --> 00:01:24.037
Losing the receptor and losing the ligand are not the same.

21
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:30.700
Predict again. A normal cell sits beside cells with less Notch activity than its

22
00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:32.560
own. What does it become?

23
00:01:38.983 --> 00:01:40.463
It became epidermis.

24
00:01:41.623 --> 00:01:45.823
Beside cells with more Notch activity than its own, the same cell became a neural

25
00:01:45.823 --> 00:01:46.823
precursor.

26
00:01:47.433 --> 00:01:52.553
In these mosaics, a cell's fate depended on its Notch activity relative to the cells

27
00:01:52.553 --> 00:01:53.553
beside it.

28
00:01:54.067 --> 00:01:57.827
Take the pair from chapter three, and give cell A half the normal Notch.

29
00:01:58.867 --> 00:02:02.367
hears its neighbour less, keeps more proneural factor, and wins.

30
00:02:03.207 --> 00:02:05.527
That is the logic of the mosaics, in miniature.

31
00:02:06.667 --> 00:02:11.547
Heitzler and Simpson argued that the cells compete, and that Notch receives an

32
00:02:11.547 --> 00:02:13.547
inhibitory signal from the precursors.

33
00:02:14.567 --> 00:02:19.607
That picture of lateral inhibition with feedback was later written as a mathematical

34
00:02:19.607 --> 00:02:20.607
model.

35
00:02:21.083 --> 00:02:24.243
That answers the second puzzle, with one qualification.

36
00:02:25.343 --> 00:02:27.523
In a sheet, the loop alone gives spaced winners.

37
00:02:28.633 --> 00:02:33.173
Neighbours compare their Notch signals, and the comparison helps decide which cell

38
00:02:33.173 --> 00:02:33.433
wins.
